<h1>Using Record to Model Immutable Data</h1>
<p>The article discusses how records simplify the creation of immutable classes in Java. Here are the key points:</p>

<h2>Calling Records to the Rescue</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Records, introduced in Java SE 14, provide a concise way to model immutable data.</li>
    <li>The compiler generates the <code>toString()</code>, <code>equals()</code>, and <code>hashCode()</code> methods, along with the canonical constructor.</li>
    <li>Records simplify code and reduce the risk of bugs.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Class of a Record</h2>
<ul>
    <li>A record is declared with the <code>record</code> keyword instead of the <code>class</code> keyword.</li>
    <li>The class created for a record is <code>final</code> and extends the <code>java.lang.Record</code> class.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Declaring the Components of a Record</h2>
<ul>
    <li>The components of a record are declared in the block that follows the record name.</li>
    <li>The compiler creates private final fields and accessors for each component.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Things you Cannot Add to a Record</h2>
<ul>
    <li>No instance fields, field initializers, or instance initializers can be added to a record.</li>
    <li>You can create static fields with initializers and static initializers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Constructing a Record with its Canonical Constructor</h2>
<ul>
    <li>The canonical constructor initializes the fields of the record.</li>
    <li>You can redefine the canonical constructor for validation or defensive copying.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Using the Compact Constructor</h2>
<p>The compact constructor syntax allows for adding validation rules.</p>

<h2>Using the Canonical Constructor</h2>
<p>The non-compact form of the canonical constructor can be used if reassignment of parameters is not preferred.</p>

<h2>Defining any Constructor</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Additional constructors can be added, but they must call the canonical constructor.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Getting the State of a Record</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Accessors for each component are generated by the compiler.</li>
    <li>Custom accessors can be defined if needed, e.g., to return a defensive copy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Serializing Records</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Records can be serialized and deserialized if they implement <code>Serializable</code>.</li>
    <li>Validation rules in the canonical constructor are enforced during deserialization.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Using Records in a Real Use Case</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Records can be used to carry data in the object model of an application.</li>
    <li>Local records can improve the readability of code, e.g., when working with data aggregates.</li>
</ul>

<p>For more details, you can check out the <a href="https://dev.java/learn/records/">full tutorial</a> on the dev.java website.</p>
